{
  "title": "Old English and Anglo-Saxon Names",
  "seoTitle": "Old English Names: 101 Anglo-Saxon Girl and Boy Names",
  "description": "Recorded Anglo-Saxon names like Æthelflæd, Eadgifu and Ælfred, and the modern names that came from them, from Audrey and Edith to Alfred and Edwin.",
  "section": "heritage",
  "published": "2026-11-13",
  "motif": "crown",
  "intro": "<p>This list has two kinds of name. The first are real Anglo-Saxon names, recorded for people who lived in England before 1066: Æthelflæd, Eadgifu, Wulfstan, Leofric. The second are names still used today that grew out of them, such as Audrey (from Æthelthryth), Edith, Alfred and Edwin, plus a few modern names made from Old English place names.</p><p>Most Anglo-Saxon names were built from two ordinary words, so the meanings here are given as their parts: Eadgifu is <em>ead</em> (prosperity) plus <em>gifu</em> (gift). We checked them against Behind the Name, the Oxford Dictionary of First Names and the Prosopography of Anglo-Saxon England.</p>",
