  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Why the meanings are given as parts",
      "content": "<p>An Anglo-Saxon name was usually two elements joined together, chosen partly from the family's stock of names. Æthelwulf of Wessex named his sons Æthelstan, Æthelbald, Æthelberht, Æthelred and Ælfred. The result did not have to make sense as a phrase, so reading Wulfstan as \"wolf-stone\" tells you what the parts mean, not what the parents meant. That is why this page writes most meanings as \"wolf + stone\" rather than turning them into a sentence.</p><p>The second element also usually showed whether a name was a woman's or a man's. Names ending in <em>-gifu</em>, <em>-gyth</em>, <em>-thryth</em>, <em>-burh</em>, <em>-flæd</em> and <em>-wynn</em> are women's; names ending in <em>-stan</em>, <em>-weard</em>, <em>-ric</em> and <em>-wine</em> are men's.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Spellings: æ, th and burh",
      "content": "<p>Old English had letters modern English dropped. <strong>Æ</strong> was a vowel like the <em>a</em> in <em>cat</em>, and the sound <em>th</em> was written with þ or ð, which we give as <em>th</em>. You will see the same name spelled several ways: Æthelthryth, Æðelþryð and Ethelthryth are one name. Some women's names end in <em>-burh</em> or <em>-burg</em>, which are two spellings of the same word for a fortress.</p><p>If you use one of these names for a child, the simplest route is a modern descendant (Audrey, Edith, Alfred) or a plain spelling such as Ethelfled or Wulfric. For a historical story, keeping the æ looks right on the page.</p>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Which names survived",
      "content": "<p>After the Norman Conquest most English families moved to names like William, Robert and Alice within a few generations. The Old English names that lasted mostly had a saint or a royal behind them: Edward the Confessor, Edmund the martyr, Audrey of Ely. Many others survived only as surnames, such as Goodwin from Godwine and Lewin from Leofwine.</p><p>The Victorians brought a batch back, including Alfred, Edgar, Edwin, Edith, Hilda and Mildred, and invented Ethel as a short form of <em>Æthel-</em> names. Modern names like Ashley, Shirley, Stanley and Bradley are Old English too, but at one remove: they began as place names, became surnames and only then became first names.</p>"
    }
  ],
